cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Smallest witnesses by girth for rad(n) | f(n)"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Ellena Godoy"
    given-names: "Jorge"
abstract: >-
  For a multiplicative function f, let S(f) be the set of integers n with
  rad(n) dividing f(n), and let the covering digraph of n have the primes
  dividing n as vertices with an edge q -> p whenever p divides f(q^e), where
  q^e is the exact power of q in n. This work computes the smallest member of
  S(f) of each girth, for f = sigma, sigma* and phi*. Any minimal witness of
  girth k has exactly k distinct prime factors and a covering digraph that is a
  pure directed cycle; its largest prime is bounded in terms of any known
  witness, which makes the enumeration finite; a per-arc strengthening of that
  bound makes the search several times cheaper; and a floor that mentions no
  witness at all, combined with doubling over an exhaustive search, removes the
  need for a known witness entirely. All nineteen published values are proved
  minimal rather than minimal among the primes examined. Five of them had not
  been computed before, and one of those, sigma* at girth 9, had no known
  witness of its girth and so could not be reached by the earlier method. All
  results are verified by independent implementations and cross-checked against
  the count of prime-abundant numbers published by Pollack and Pomerance (2012).
keywords:
  - number theory
  - arithmetic functions
  - sum of divisors
  - prime-abundant numbers
  - covering digraph
  - girth
  - integer sequences
